  "result": "**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` used a regex that only accepts plain digits, like `12.50`. When a price had a thousands separator (`\"2,450.50\"`, `\"1,299.00\"`, `\"1,005.99\"`), the match came back `null`. The code then read `m[1]` on it and threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`, which surfaced as a 500 on `POST /checkout`.\n\n**Log evidence:** Every `POST /checkout 500` I checked in `logs/app.log` had one of those comma-formatted prices in the `DEBUG checkout payload` line just before it. I read the first few in full. I only grepped for comma-formatted prices; I did not count the 500s or confirm that every one of them has such a price. That is why it looked intermittent: it only happens on orders containing an item priced at 1,000 or more.\n\n**Fix in `src/money.js`:**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts comma-grouped thousands (`1,234` or `2,450.50`).\n- Input that isn't a valid amount now throws `Error('Invalid amount: <input>')`, as the function's own comment already promised, instead of a `TypeError`.\n- This does not change the HTTP status for invalid input. A bad price still throws, and I didn't check whether `src/server.js` turns that into a 4xx or a 500.\n\n**Regression tests in `test/money.test.js`:**\n- Parsing the exact prices from the log.\n- Rejecting malformed amounts such as `abc`, an empty string, `1,2,3`, `12.345` and `1,29.00`.\n- A `checkout` test with a `\"2,450.50\"` item.\n\n`npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests, including the 3 new ones. I haven't committed anything.",
